sum='Part I: Santosh Yadav was born in Joniyawas village (Rewari, Haryana) in a society that did not favour girls. She refused to wear traditional dresses, refused early marriage and insisted on studying; she left for Delhi and joined a school, saying she would earn by part-time jobs, so her parents agreed to pay. From Kasturba Hostel (Jaipur, Maharani College) she watched villagers climb the Aravalli hills and vanish – she found they were mountaineers. She joined Uttarkashi’s Nehru Institute of Mountaineering, climbed Everest in 1992 and again in 1993 – the only woman to climb it twice. She saved a fellow climber Mohan Singh by sharing oxygen, received the Padmashri, and brought down 500 kg of garbage from the Himalayas. Part II: Maria Sharapova from Siberia was sent to Florida, USA, at nine for tennis training; her mother stayed back because of visa restrictions. Lonely and humiliated by older pupils, she became mentally tough. She won Wimbledon in 2004 and became world No. 1 on 22 August 2005. She is proudly Russian and says money is a motivation, but being No. 1 is her dream.',
